[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"UML Diagrams in Core Java: Class, Sequence, and Use Case Diagrams"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unified Modeling Language (UML) diagrams are a standardized way to visualize the design of a system. They provide a blueprint for developers, facilitating communication and ensuring a common understanding of the system's architecture. In Core Java development, UML diagrams are invaluable for designing robust and maintainable applications. This tutorial will cover three essential UML diagram types: Class Diagrams, Sequence Diagrams, and Use Case Diagrams."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Class Diagrams"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Class diagrams are the most common type of UML diagram. They describe the static structure of a system by showing the classes, their attributes, methods, and the relationships between them. They are essential for object-oriented design."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"1.1 Elements of a Class Diagram"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Class:** Represented by a rectangle divided into three sections: class name, attributes, and methods."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Attributes:** Represent the data held by the class. They include name, type, and visibility (public, private, protected)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Methods:** Represent the operations the class can perform. They include name, parameters, return type, and visibility."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Relationships:** Represent the connections between classes. Common relationships include association, aggregation, composition, inheritance (generalization), and realization (interface implementation)."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"1.2 Relationships in Detail"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Association:** A general relationship indicating that classes are related. Represented by a solid line. Can be unidirectional or bidirectional. Multiplicity can be indicated (e.g., 1..*, 0..1)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Aggregation:** A 'has-a' relationship where the part can exist independently of the whole. Represented by a line with an empty diamond at the whole end."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Composition:** A stronger 'has-a' relationship where the part cannot exist independently of the whole. Represented by a line with a filled diamond at the whole end."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Inheritance (Generalization):** An 'is-a' relationship where a subclass inherits properties and behaviors from a superclass. Represented by a line with an empty triangle at the superclass end."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Realization (Interface Implementation):** A class implements an interface. Represented by a dashed line with an empty triangle at the interface end."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"1.3 Example: Class Diagram for a Library System"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's consider a simplified library system. We can identify classes like `Book`, `Library`, `Member`, and `Loan`. The diagram would show their attributes (e.g., `Book` has `title`, `author`, `ISBN`) and methods (e.g., `Library` has `addBook`, `removeBook`, `lendBook`). The relationships would show how these classes interact (e.g., `Library` has many `Book`s - aggregation, `Member` borrows `Book`s via `Loan` - association)."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"class Book {
    private String title;
    private String author;
    private String ISBN;

    public Book(String title, String author, String ISBN) {
        this.title = title;
        this.author = author;
        this.ISBN = ISBN;
    }

    // Getters and setters
}

class Library {
    private List<Book> books = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addBook(Book book) {
        books.add(book);
    }

    public void removeBook(Book book) {
        books.remove(book);
    }

    // Other methods
}

class Member {
    private String memberId;
    private String name;

    // Getters and setters
}

class Loan {
    private Book book;
    private Member member;
    private Date loanDate;
    private Date returnDate;

    // Getters and setters
}"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code snippet represents the classes and their basic attributes. A class diagram would visually represent these classes and their relationships. For example, the `Library` class would have an aggregation relationship with the `Book` class (Library has Books)."},{"order":20,"type":"heading","text":"2. Sequence Diagrams"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sequence diagrams illustrate how objects interact with each other in a specific scenario over time. They focus on the order of messages passed between objects. They are useful for understanding the dynamic behavior of a system."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"2.1 Elements of a Sequence Diagram"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Objects:** Represented by rectangles at the top of the diagram with a lifeline (dashed vertical line) extending downwards."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Lifeline:** Represents the existence of an object over time."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Activation Box:** A rectangle on the lifeline indicating when the object is active (executing a method)."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Messages:** Represented by arrows between lifelines, indicating a method call. Can be synchronous (solid arrow) or asynchronous (dashed arrow)."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Return Messages:** Represented by dashed arrows, indicating the return value of a method call."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"2.2 Example: Sequence Diagram for Borrowing a Book"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider the scenario where a `Member` borrows a `Book` from a `Library`. The sequence diagram would show the `Member` object sending a `borrowBook` message to the `Library` object. The `Library` object might then interact with a `Loan` object to record the loan. The diagram would illustrate the order of these interactions."},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"// Simplified code illustrating the interaction

public class Library {
    public void borrowBook(Member member, Book book) {
        if (isBookAvailable(book)) {
            Loan loan = new Loan(book, member, new Date());
            // ... record the loan
            System.out.println("Book borrowed successfully.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Book is not available.");
        }
    }

    private boolean isBookAvailable(Book book) {
        // Logic to check book availability
        return true; // Placeholder
    }
}

public class Member {
    public void requestBorrow(Library library, Book book) {
        library.borrowBook(this, book);
    }
}"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"The sequence diagram would show the `Member` object calling `requestBorrow` on itself, which then calls `borrowBook` on the `Library` object. The `Library` object then creates a `Loan` object. The diagram visually represents this flow of method calls."},{"order":32,"type":"heading","text":"3. Use Case Diagrams"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use case diagrams describe the functionality of a system from the user's perspective. They show the actors (users or external systems) and the use cases (actions the actors can perform). They are useful for defining the scope of a project and understanding user requirements."},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"3.1 Elements of a Use Case Diagram"},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Actor:** Represents a user or external system that interacts with the system. Represented by a stick figure."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Use Case:** Represents a specific action or goal that an actor can achieve with the system. Represented by an ellipse."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**System Boundary:** Represents the scope of the system. Represented by a rectangle enclosing the use cases."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Relationships:** Represent the connections between actors and use cases, and between use cases themselves (e.g., include, extend)."},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"3.2 Relationships in Detail (Use Case Diagrams)"},{"order":40,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Association:**  A line connecting an actor to a use case, indicating that the actor participates in that use case."},{"order":41,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Include:** A use case includes the functionality of another use case. Represented by a dashed arrow labeled '<<include>>'."},{"order":42,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Extend:** A use case extends the functionality of another use case under certain conditions. Represented by a dashed arrow labeled '<<extend>>'."},{"order":43,"type":"subheading","text":"3.3 Example: Use Case Diagram for a Library System"},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the library system, actors might include `Member` and `Librarian`. Use cases might include `Borrow Book`, `Return Book`, `Search Book`, and `Add Book` (for the librarian).  A use case diagram would show the actors and the use cases they participate in. For example, the `Member` actor would be associated with `Borrow Book`, `Return Book`, and `Search Book` use cases. The `Librarian` actor would be associated with `Add Book`, `Remove Book`, and potentially `Search Book`."},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"There's no direct code equivalent for a Use Case diagram, as it primarily represents user interactions and system boundaries. However, the use cases inform the development of the system's functionality."},{"order":46,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":47,"type":"paragraph","text":"UML diagrams are powerful tools for designing and documenting software systems. Class diagrams define the static structure, sequence diagrams illustrate dynamic behavior, and use case diagrams capture user requirements. By mastering these three diagram types, Core Java developers can create more robust, maintainable, and user-friendly applications."}]
